      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/abbyshade111/SecureVibe",
          "name": "sv",
          "rules": [
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "If any part of that value can be influenced from outside, whoever influences it chooses what the app does next."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Work out what the code is actually for. A lookup table, a match on a fixed set of names, or a small parser will do the job without running text as code."
              },
              "id": "ast.dynamic-code-execution",
              "name": "ast.dynamic-code-execution",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V1.3.2"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-95",
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Code is built at run time and executed"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "If any part of the path comes from a request, a name like ../../etc/passwd reads or overwrites a file outside the folder the app meant to use."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Keep user-submitted file names out of file paths: store files under names the app generates, and look them up by id. Where a submitted name has to be used, reduce it to a bare name (secure_filename in Flask, path.basename in Node) and check that the resolved path is still inside the folder it should be in."
              },
              "id": "ast.file-path-from-value",
              "name": "ast.file-path-from-value",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V5.3.2"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-22",
                  "CWE-73"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A file is opened at a path built from a value"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "If the destination comes from the request, as a ?next= parameter usually does, a link on the app's own domain can send somebody to a lookalike sign-in page on another domain, and the address they clicked was genuine."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Redirect only to the app's own routes, built with its router (url_for, reverse, a Rails _path helper), or check the destination against a short list of allowed hosts. For a return-to address, accept only a path that starts with a single / and not //."
              },
              "id": "ast.open-redirect",
              "name": "ast.open-redirect",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V3.7.2"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-601"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "The app redirects to a destination built from a value"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A semicolon or a backtick in the wrong place turns one command into two, and the second one is whatever the attacker wanted."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Pass the program and its arguments as a list instead of a string, so the shell never parses them. Where a shell is genuinely needed, validate the value against a fixed set first."
              },
              "id": "ast.shell-command",
              "name": "ast.shell-command",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V1.2.5"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A shell command is built from a value"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Whatever ends up inside the backticks is run by a shell, so a semicolon in someone's input starts a second command with the app's own permissions."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Run the program directly with its arguments as a list, rather than building one string for a shell to pull apart."
              },
              "id": "ast.shell-command-backticks",
              "name": "ast.shell-command-backticks",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V1.2.5"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-78"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A command is run with backticks, built from text"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "A quote in someone's name is enough to change what the query does, and a deliberate one can read or delete any table the app can reach."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Send the query with placeholders and pass the values alongside it. Every database library supports this, and it is shorter than building the string."
              },
              "id": "ast.sql-built-by-hand",
              "name": "ast.sql-built-by-hand",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V1.2.4"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-89"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A database query is built by joining text together"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Reading the data is enough to run code: the attacker does not need the app to use the result, only to load it."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use the safe reader — yaml.safe_load rather than yaml.load — and prefer a format that carries data only, such as JSON, for anything that arrives from outside."
              },
              "id": "ast.unsafe-deserialization",
              "name": "ast.unsafe-deserialization",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V1.5.2"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-502"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Data from outside is turned back into objects unsafely"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ECB leaves the shape of the data visible through the encryption, and the retired ciphers can be broken or have known practical attacks, so the encrypted data is less private than it looks."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use AES with GCM (or ChaCha20-Poly1305) through your language's high-level library, which chooses the mode and the nonce for you. In Java, always name the mode: Cipher.getInstance(\"AES\") quietly means ECB."
              },
              "id": "ast.weak-cipher",
              "name": "ast.weak-cipher",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V11.3.1",
                  "V11.3.2"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A cipher or block mode that is not approved is used"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Anything that relies on the hash to tell two things apart, or to prove something was not changed, can be fooled by somebody who builds a second input with the same hash. Used for passwords, both are also fast enough to guess through quickly."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "Use SHA-256 or stronger for integrity and signatures, and a password hashing function (Argon2id, scrypt, bcrypt) for passwords. If this hash is only a cache key or a file checksum with no security purpose, say so where it is used; Python lets you pass usedforsecurity=False."
              },
              "id": "ast.weak-hash-function",
              "name": "ast.weak-hash-function",
              "properties": {
                "requirements": [
                  "V11.4.1"
                ],
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-328",
                  "CWE-327"
                ]
              },
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "A disallowed hash function is used"
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Reported by Bandit, which reads python the way its own community has learned to."
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "See Bandit's documentation for rule B101."
              },
              "id": "bandit.B101",
              "name": "bandit.B101",
              "properties": {
